### Group Playback with Howler.js - JavaScript

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

Demonstrates creating a new Howl instance with audio sprites and controlling multiple concurrently playing sounds from that instance. It shows how to play individual sprite tracks by name, control the volume of the entire group, and pause all currently playing sounds within the group using global instance methods.

```javascript
var sound = new Howl({
  src: ['sound.webm', 'sound.mp3'],
  sprite: {
    track01: [0, 20000],
    track02: [21000, 41000]
  }
});

// Play each of the track.s
sound.play('track01');
sound.play('track02');

// Change the volume of both tracks.
sound.volume(0.5);

// After a second, pause both sounds in the group.
setTimeout(function() {
  sound.pause();
}, 1000);
```

--------------------------------

### Configuring Howl with playback options

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

This example demonstrates initializing a Howl object with several common options such as multiple sources, autoplay, looping, volume, and an event callback for when the sound finishes.

```javascript
var sound = new Howl({
  src: ['sound.webm', 'sound.mp3', 'sound.wav'],
  autoplay: true,
  loop: true,
  volume: 0.5,
  onend: function() {
    console.log('Finished!');
  }
});
```

--------------------------------

### Using Howler.js with ES6 syntax

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

This example combines ES6 module import with creating a Howl object and demonstrates controlling the global volume for all sounds using the Howler object.

```javascript
import {Howl, Howler} from 'howler';

// Setup the new Howl.
const sound = new Howl({
  src: ['sound.webm', 'sound.mp3']
});

// Play the sound.
sound.play();

// Change global volume.
Howler.volume(0.5);
```

--------------------------------

### Playing a basic sound with Howler.js

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

This is the most basic example showing how to create a Howl object with a single source file and immediately play it using the play() method.

```javascript
var sound = new Howl({
  src: ['sound.mp3']
});

sound.play();
```

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

Description > Features: * Single API for all audio needs
* Defaults to Web Audio API and falls back to HTML5 Audio
* Handles edge cases and bugs across environments
* Supports all codecs for full cross-browser support
* Automatic caching for improved performance
* Control sounds individually, in groups or globally
* Playback of multiple sounds at once
* Easy sound sprite definition and playback
* Full control for fading, rate, seek, volume, etc.
* Easily add 3D spatial sound or stereo panning
* Modular - use what you want and easy to extend
* No outside dependencies, just pure JavaScript
* As light as 7kb gzipped

### Muting/Unmuting All Sounds howler.js JavaScript

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md

Mutes or unmutes all sounds managed by howler.js globally. This method replaces the old global `unmute` method. It takes a boolean argument: `true` to mute everything, `false` to unmute everything.

```javascript
Howler.mute(true);
```

--------------------------------

### Using Howler.js with ES6 syntax

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

This example combines ES6 module import with creating a Howl object and demonstrates controlling the global volume for all sounds using the Howler object.

```javascript
import {Howl, Howler} from 'howler';

// Setup the new Howl.
const sound = new Howl({
  src: ['sound.webm', 'sound.mp3']
});

// Play the sound.
sound.play();

// Change global volume.
Howler.volume(0.5);
```

--------------------------------

### Disable Howler.js Auto Unlock - JavaScript

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

Shows how to disable the default behavior of howler.js, which attempts to silently unlock audio playback on mobile browsers and Chrome/Safari by playing an empty buffer on the first `touchend` event. Setting `Howler.autoUnlock` to `false` prevents this automatic unlock mechanism.

```javascript
Howler.autoUnlock = false;
```

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/CHANGELOG.md

2.0.0 (July 19, 2016): The biggest change is how you should think about your audio when using howler.js. There is now the concept of global (`Howler`), group (`Howl`) and single sound (`Sound`). Each sound that is played gets its own `Sound` object that can be manipulated, giving much greater control over playback, whether using sprites or not. `Howl` method calls can then apply to one sound or all in the group.

--------------------------------

Source: https://github.com/goldfire/howler.js/blob/master/README.md

Documentation > Core > Global Options > autoSuspend `Boolean` `true`: Automatically suspends the Web Audio AudioContext after 30 seconds of inactivity to decrease processing and energy usage. Automatically resumes upon new playback. Set this property to `false` to disable this behavior.

